  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14415v1</id>
    <title>Red-Teaming of Vision-Language-Action Models via Quality Diversity Environment Generation</title>
    <updated>2026-10-10T00:25:36Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14415v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.14415v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>A long-standing goal in robotics is to develop generalist robots that can perform tasks across a wide range of settings, flexibly specified with images and text. Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer a promising path toward this goal by training on large collections of robot demonstrations. However, while VLAs perform well in-distribution, they often fail under minor test-time shifts, such as changes in object positions, lighting, textures, and camera poses. To address this, we propose Quality Diversity for Diverse Environment Generation (Q-DEG), a method for improving VLA robustness by generating adversarial environments and fine-tuning the VLA on its own successful rollouts. Q-DEG uses Quality Diversity (QD) optimization to find a set of high-failure environments, while promoting diversity using policy embeddings extracted from the VLA. These environments expose vulnerabilities that can be mitigated through self-imitation fine-tuning, thereby improving the VLA's robustness. In simulation, we conduct experiments across three representative VLA models, and our results indicate that Q-DEG improves robustness to out-of-distribution (OOD) adversarial environments compared to the base VLA and VLAs fine-tuned using data collected from two red-teaming baselines. Furthermore, we demonstrate that improving VLA robustness through Q-DEG facilitates sim-to-real transfer. Our anonymous project website is at: https://qd-env.github.io/qdeg/.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
